From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 22:25:49 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] um: add an mconsole sync command

The host asks a guest to write its file systems back before it stops
running the guest, to hold the guest's dirty pages in its own hands.
Its only request was SysRq 's', which is refused whenever the guest's
SysRq mask is off and the command line did not set
sysrq_always_enabled, and which emergency_sync() runs asynchronously:
the console replies when the writeback has started, so the host slept
for a fixed time and hoped.

Add a "sync" command that runs ksys_sync(), as sync(2) does, and
replies "synced in <n> ms" only once it has returned.  It syncs the
inodes, the file systems and the block devices, whose flush the UBD
driver carries to the host's descriptor, and needs no SysRq mask.
Suspend runs the same ksys_sync() but only when sync_on_suspend is set,
and replies after it, so the command also gives a host that cannot
suspend the guest the durability that suspend had.

The sync runs on a work item of its own: the console runs its
process-context requests one at a time, and one that waits, as when the
host holds a block device while its disk is full, would hold up a trim,
a suspend and every other request behind it.  The reply is the only
completion signal, a sync that cannot finish sends none, and the host
bounds its own wait.  An older kernel answers "Unknown command".

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/um/drivers/mconsole.h      |  1 +
 arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c | 48 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 arch/um/drivers/mconsole_user.c |  1 +
 3 files changed, 50 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/um/drivers/mconsole.h b/arch/um/drivers/mconsole.h
index bbaaffc484eb218acefffdffbc15cd7e5976d807..ae489e643cd377f1d07f0336e2f36bbf8ec17d6b 100644
--- a/arch/um/drivers/mconsole.h
+++ b/arch/um/drivers/mconsole.h
@@ -94,6 +94,7 @@ extern void mconsole_proc(struct mc_request *req);
 extern void mconsole_stack(struct mc_request *req);
 extern void mconsole_mem(struct mc_request *req);
 extern void mconsole_trim(struct mc_request *req);
+extern void mconsole_sync(struct mc_request *req);
 
 extern int mconsole_get_request(int fd, struct mc_request *req);
 extern int mconsole_notify(char *sock_name, int type, const void *data,
diff --git a/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c b/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c
index 57979cb49b0f642beb61e01f6f5ce4722a8dd036..d9753c60ac429c5b1fdb3a556c38934c89112357 100644
--- a/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c
+++ b/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_kern.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/string.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
+#include <linux/ktime.h>
 #include <linux/list.h>
 #include <linux/mm.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
@@ -229,6 +230,8 @@ void mconsole_proc(struct mc_request *req)
     mem - returns the memory total=, available= and free=, in bytes\n\
     trim on|off|once - trim page cache the host already caches:\n\
 	periodically, no longer, or all of the cold cache now\n\
+    sync - write every file system back, as sync(2) does, and reply when\n\
+	that has returned\n\
 "
 
 void mconsole_help(struct mc_request *req)
@@ -940,6 +943,51 @@ void mconsole_trim(struct mc_request *req)
 	}
 }
 
+struct mconsole_sync {
+	struct work_struct work;
+	struct mc_request req;
+};
+
+static void mconsole_sync_work(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct mconsole_sync *sync =
+		container_of(work, struct mconsole_sync, work);
+	ktime_t start = ktime_get();
+	char reply[32];
+
+	ksys_sync();
+	snprintf(reply, sizeof(reply), "synced in %lld ms",
+		 ktime_ms_delta(ktime_get(), start));
+	mconsole_reply(&sync->req, reply, 0, 0);
+	kfree(sync);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Write every file system and block device back, as sync(2) does, and reply
+ * once that has returned.  The host asks before it stops running the guest, to
+ * have the guest's dirty pages in its hands.  SysRq 's' only starts the
+ * writeback and is refused while the SysRq mask is off; this does neither.
+ *
+ * The sync runs on a work item of its own, so one that has to wait, for a
+ * block device the host holds while its disk is full or for a FUSE daemon
+ * that does not answer, delays no other console request.  It sends no reply
+ * until it has returned, and the host bounds its own wait.  The reply says
+ * the pass returned.  sync(2) reports no I/O error, so one that a file
+ * system lost is not reported either.
+ */
+void mconsole_sync(struct mc_request *req)
+{
+	struct mconsole_sync *sync = kmalloc(sizeof(*sync), GFP_KERNEL);
+
+	if (sync == NULL) {
+		mconsole_reply(req, "Out of memory", 1, 0);
+		return;
+	}
+	sync->req = *req;
+	INIT_WORK(&sync->work, mconsole_sync_work);
+	queue_work(system_unbound_wq, &sync->work);
+}
+
 static int __init mount_proc(void)
 {
 	struct file_system_type *proc_fs_type;
diff --git a/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_user.c b/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_user.c
index 6ead5ac46da71436c324b8579e0b853d52964616..d55ae3ac7fde06c643eda8ad1d919ec54dc9628a 100644
--- a/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_user.c
+++ b/arch/um/drivers/mconsole_user.c
@@ -36,6 +36,7 @@ static struct mconsole_command commands[] = {
 	{ "stack", mconsole_stack, MCONSOLE_INTR },
 	{ "mem", mconsole_mem, MCONSOLE_INTR },
 	{ "trim", mconsole_trim, MCONSOLE_PROC },
+	{ "sync", mconsole_sync, MCONSOLE_PROC },
 };
 
 /* Initialized in mconsole_init, which is an initcall */
